    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We need to find the product of the matrices\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\dotsm \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nFirst, let's compute the product of two consecutive matrices. Consider the product of two general matrices\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\text{ and } \\begin{pmatrix} e & f \\\\ g & h \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe multiplication of these matrices yields\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} e & f \\\\ g & h \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} ae + bg & af + bh \\\\ ce + dg & cf + dh \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nApplying the matrices in the given sequence to themselves, we get\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 0 & 1 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 1 \\\\ 0 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 0 & 0 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis indicates that when multiplying the matrices in the sequence, a distinct matrix will be obtained by the end. Therefore, the entire product of all matrices\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\dotsm \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nwill result in the final matrix\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 5000 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]",
